      <h1>Dog Bite Lawyer California: Who Pays After a Bite</h1>
      <div class="entry-content">
<p>Under California law, the owner of a dog is liable when the dog bites someone, even if the dog has never bitten anyone before. The one bite rule does not apply to bite claims in California. You do not have to prove the owner knew the dog was dangerous. You have to show you were bitten in a public place, or while lawfully in a private place.</p>
<p>Most people who search for a dog bite lawyer in California have already been told the opposite. A neighbor says the first bite is free. An adjuster says the dog has no history. Both are describing other states.</p>
<p>Who pays is a separate question from who is liable. The money usually comes from the owner's homeowners or renters liability policy, up to the policy limit, not from the owner personally.</p>
<h2 id="what-strict-liability-means-under-california-dog-bite-law">What strict liability means under California dog bite law</h2>
<p><a href="https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displaySection.xhtml?lawCode=CIV&amp;sectionNum=3342">California Civil Code § 3342</a> says the owner of a dog is liable for damages if the dog bites a person in a public place, or while that person is lawfully in a private place, including the owner's property. Then comes the line that decides most cases: this applies regardless of the former viciousness of the dog or the owner's knowledge of that viciousness.</p>
<p>Strict liability means three things:</p>
<ul><li><strong>The dog bit you.</strong> Teeth made contact and the bite caused injury. A bite through a sleeve that leaves a bruise or a crush injury still counts.</li><li><strong>You were allowed to be there.</strong> A public place, or private property where you had permission.</li><li><strong>You suffered damages.</strong> Medical bills, lost income, pain, scarring, or all of them.</li></ul>
<p>If those three are covered, the owner's carelessness is not the question, and you do not have to prove anyone knew the dog was dangerous. Your own conduct can still reduce an award under comparative fault, but it does not create an element you have to prove.</p>
<h3 id="why-the-one-bite-rule-keeps-coming-up">Why the one bite rule keeps coming up</h3>
<p>The one bite rule is old common law, and several states still follow it. It gave a dog one free bite, since the owner could not know the dog was dangerous. California replaced it with this statute. The "beware of dog" sign fits the same myth. A sign may shape a negligence argument, but it does not defeat a claim under § 3342.</p>
<h2 id="who-can-be-liable-for-a-dog-bite-in-california">Who can be liable for a dog bite in California</h2>
<p>The statute names the owner, so start there.</p>
<ul><li><strong>The owner.</strong> Strictly liable under § 3342. Whoever holds title, pays the license and controls the animal.</li><li><strong>A keeper or handler.</strong> Someone who keeps or harbors the dog can be treated as an owner. Someone who only walks it is more likely facing an ordinary negligence claim.</li><li><strong>A landlord.</strong> This is where people get carried away. A landlord is not liable just because a tenant owns a dog. Liability is narrow. It usually needs two things: the landlord knew the dog had dangerous habits, and the landlord had the power to do something about it, such as enforcing a lease term or acting on a hazard they were told about.</li></ul>
<p>Standard homeowners policies usually include personal liability coverage that responds to a bite, and many renters policies do too. That coverage is often the only money available.</p>
<h2 id="bites-and-non-bite-injuries-follow-different-legal-routes">Bites and non-bite injuries follow different legal routes</h2>
<p>This distinction decides real cases. A dog that knocks you down on a sidewalk did not bite you, and Section 3342 does not cover that on its face. Those injuries proceed as ordinary negligence: you must show the owner was careless, and usually that the owner knew or should have known the dog had a tendency to behave that way.</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Injury type</th><th>Legal route</th><th>What must be proven</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Bite, with or without broken skin</td><td>Cal. Civ. Code § 3342, strict liability</td><td>A bite occurred, you were lawfully present, and you suffered damages</td></tr><tr><td>Dog knocks you down, no bite</td><td>Ordinary negligence</td><td>Duty, unreasonable conduct, and knowledge of the dog's tendency</td></tr><tr><td>Dog causes a fall, bike crash or car crash</td><td>Ordinary negligence</td><td>Same as above, plus a clear link between the dog's behavior and your injury</td></tr><tr><td>Bite by a police or agency dog on duty</td><td>Government claim and immunity rules</td><td>A formal government claim on the statutory timeline, plus immunity rules, and a real question about whether you were lawfully present during an arrest</td></tr><tr><td>Injury at a rental property</td><td>Owner claim first, landlord claim only in narrow cases</td><td>The bite route, plus proof the landlord controlled the property and knew of the danger</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<h2 id="what-a-dog-bite-claim-can-pay-for">What a dog bite claim can pay for</h2>
<p><strong>Medical care, now and later.</strong> Emergency treatment, wound care, antibiotics, stitches, follow-up. Dog bites infect easily, so treatment often continues after the wound looks closed. Scar revision and therapy for nerve damage may come years later.</p>
<p><strong>Scarring and disfigurement.</strong> Bites land on hands, forearms, calves and faces. A facial scar on a child is a long-term item.</p>
<p><strong>Lost wages and lost earning capacity.</strong> Time off for treatment, and any lasting limit on the work you can do. A chef with a damaged dominant hand is a different claim from a desk worker bitten on the calf.</p>
<p><strong>Pain and suffering.</strong> The bite, the fear, the nights you could not sleep, the way you now cross the street when you see a loose dog. Our guide to <a href="/blog/how-pain-and-suffering-damages-are-calculated/">how pain and suffering damages are calculated</a> explains how that number is built.</p>
<p>California's cap on non-economic damages in medical malpractice cases, known as MICRA, does not apply to dog bites. And no published "average dog bite settlement" is worth relying on. Those figures mix minor bites with disfiguring attacks, hide dismissed cases, and cluster around policy limits.</p>
<h2 id="evidence-that-decides-dog-bite-cases">Evidence that decides dog bite cases</h2>
<p>The statute makes liability simple. Proof is where cases are won, and most of it disappears quietly.</p>
<ol><li><strong>Photograph the wound on day one, then weekly.</strong> Keep a dated set. Bites look bad early and worse later.</li><li><strong>Identify the dog and owner precisely.</strong> Name, address, phone, breed, color. Photograph the dog if it is safe.</li><li><strong>Get the vaccination record</strong> from the owner and from animal control.</li><li><strong>Report the bite.</strong> A same-day police or animal control report is hard to attack later.</li><li><strong>Collect witnesses.</strong> Neighbors who saw the dog act aggressively before are valuable, and they move away.</li><li><strong>Follow the treatment plan</strong> and keep every record. Gaps in care are the most common way a claim shrinks.</li><li><strong>Do not post about it.</strong> A photo of you hiking at two weeks will be used against you.</li></ol>
<h2 id="the-insurance-claim-is-usually-against-a-policy-not-the-owner">The insurance claim is usually against a policy, not the owner</h2>
<p>Dog bite claims are usually paid by a homeowners or renters liability policy, not the owner's bank account. That cuts both ways. Coverage exists without a lawsuit, but the money stops at the policy limit, and the adjuster's job is to pay as little as the file allows.</p>
<ul><li><strong>The adjuster may ask for a recorded statement.</strong> You are not required to give one.</li><li><strong>Do not sign a release or cash a check</strong> before treatment is finished. Once you sign, the claim is closed, even if the scar needs revision next year.</li><li><strong>Do not accept the first number.</strong> Opening offers are often set below the medical bills. Our post on the <a href="/blog/signs-you-need-a-lawyer-after-a-slip-and-fall-accident/">signs you need a lawyer after an injury</a> explains the pattern.</li><li><strong>Ask about every policy in the household</strong>, including an umbrella. Breed and dog bite exclusions exist, so read the declarations page.</li></ul>
<p>If the owner has no insurance and no assets, it is better to know early. It does not make the claim fake. It changes what is realistic.</p>
<h2 id="the-two-year-deadline-and-why-waiting-costs-you">The two-year deadline and why waiting costs you</h2>
<p>Under <a href="https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displaySection.xhtml?lawCode=CCP&amp;sectionNum=335.1">Code of Civil Procedure § 335.1</a>, you generally have two years from the date of injury to file a lawsuit in California. Miss it and the claim usually dies, however clear the liability.</p>
<p>Two exceptions matter. If a public agency owned or handled the dog, such as a police or animal control dog, the claim runs against the agency, not a private owner. California requires a formal government claim, on a much shorter timeline than two years, before you can sue a public entity, and immunity rules apply on top of that. Those cases need review within days, not months.</p>
<p>If the injured person was a child, the deadline is usually extended, but the practical timeline is not. Evidence fades, and the dog and its owner move.</p>
<h2 id="defenses-owners-and-insurers-raise">Defenses owners and insurers raise</h2>
<ul><li><strong>"You were trespassing."</strong> The statute requires lawful presence. If you were in a fenced yard you had no business being in, expect a fight.</li><li><strong>"You provoked the dog."</strong> Provocation is not in the statute, but your conduct can reduce an award under comparative fault. Insurers stretch this. Reaching for a dropped phone is not provocation.</li><li><strong>"It was not a bite."</strong> For a knock-down, the insurer will argue § 3342 does not apply, and often they are right.</li><li><strong>"We are not the owner."</strong> Common where the dog belongs to a roommate. The answer is usually control, care, and who the household treats as responsible.</li><li><strong>"The policy excludes this breed or loss."</strong> Read the policy. Some exclusions are valid, and some are narrower than the adjuster claims.</li></ul>
<h2 id="when-to-call-a-dog-bite-lawyer-in-california">When to call a dog bite lawyer in California</h2>
<p>You do not need a lawyer for every nip. A single stitch, a full recovery and a cooperative insurer can be handled directly.</p>
<p>Call when the wound is on the face or hand, there is a scar, a child was bitten, the owner is uninsured or evasive, or the injury came from a knock-down rather than a bite. Those are the cases where the legal route is in dispute.</p>
