<p>This is general information, not legal advice about your case. Facts change the analysis, and the deadline does not wait.</p>
<h2 id="frequently-asked-questions">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
<h3 id="does-california-have-a-one-bite-rule-for-dogs">Does California have a one bite rule for dogs?</h3>
<p>No. California is a strict liability state for dog bites under Civil Code § 3342. The owner is liable regardless of the dog's former viciousness or the owner's knowledge of it. The one bite rule is a doctrine from other states.</p>
<h3 id="who-pays-a-dog-bite-claim-in-california">Who pays a dog bite claim in California?</h3>
<p>Usually the owner's homeowners or renters insurance, up to the policy limit. The claim goes against the liability coverage, not the owner's wallet. If there is no insurance, or the injury exceeds the limit, look at other household policies, an umbrella, or the owner's assets.</p>
<h3 id="what-if-the-dog-owner-rents-and-has-no-renters-insurance">What if the dog owner rents and has no renters insurance?</h3>
<p>Your options narrow but do not disappear. The owner is still legally responsible, and a judgment can follow them. Check whether another household member has coverage, whether the dog was kept elsewhere, and whether a landlord controlled the property and knew of the danger.</p>
<h3 id="can-i-recover-if-the-dog-knocked-me-down-but-did-not-bite-me">Can I recover if the dog knocked me down but did not bite me?</h3>
<p>Possibly, but the route changes. Section 3342 covers bites. A knock-down is pursued as negligence, which means showing the owner failed to act reasonably and knew or should have known the dog had a tendency to jump, charge or knock people over.</p>
<h3 id="how-long-do-i-have-to-file-a-dog-bite-claim-in-california">How long do I have to file a dog bite claim in California?</h3>
<p>Generally two years from the date of injury under Code of Civil Procedure § 335.1. Claims against a public agency are far shorter and require a formal government claim first. Children usually have more time, but evidence and treatment should not wait.</p>
<h3 id="what-is-the-average-dog-bite-settlement-in-california">What is the average dog bite settlement in California?</h3>
<p>There is no reliable average. Published figures mix minor bites with disfiguring attacks, leave out cases that were dismissed, and cluster around insurance policy limits. What drives value is the injury, the scar, the care still ahead, lost earnings and the size of the policy, not the breed or the county. A number from a website tells you nothing about your case.</p>
